WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.000
Who does what.

2
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.934
This matrix is adapted from the Federal
Modernization Workbook's Book 08's

3
00:00:05.934 --> 00:00:09.992
responsibility matrix, which
separates deciding from doing:

4
00:00:09.992 --> 00:00:13.812
D decides and is accountable, R
does the work, C is consulted.

5
00:00:13.812 --> 00:00:16.762
I've added a Division column
for the delegated model.

6
00:00:16.762 --> 00:00:21.903
The CIO decides the platform and deployment
model, NIOS, the SaaS, or hybrid,

7
00:00:21.903 --> 00:00:27.212
with the central DDI team doing the
evaluation and everyone else consulted.

8
00:00:27.212 --> 00:00:30.560
The authorizing official decides
whether a cloud-delivered

9
00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:33.712
management plane is authorized
at the right impact level.

10
00:00:33.712 --> 00:00:38.460
The CIO also decides how divisions
are scoped in the policy plane,

11
00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.912
and the central DDI team builds it: admin
groups, network views, access views.

12
00:00:43.912 --> 00:00:48.676
Divisions are consulted, because a scope
they can't work in becomes a workaround.

13
00:00:48.676 --> 00:00:51.789
Central DDI owns the
top-level address blocks

14
00:00:51.789 --> 00:00:54.575
and namespace design, deciding and doing.

15
00:00:54.575 --> 00:00:59.767
DNSSEC signing and key rollover, and
response policy and Threat Defense policy,

16
00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:04.802
are control requirements, so the CISO
decides them and central DDI runs them,

17
00:01:04.802 --> 00:01:08.500
with security operations
consulted on response policy.

18
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.975
Standard record changes in a division's
zone are where delegation pays off.

19
00:01:12.975 --> 00:01:17.864
Central decided in advance what a standard
change is; the division does the work,

20
00:01:17.864 --> 00:01:21.938
inside its scope, through the
approval workflow where one applies.

21
00:01:21.938 --> 00:01:27.094
High-impact changes, such as zones,
NS and SRV records, apex records,

22
00:01:27.094 --> 00:01:33.301
wildcards and RPZ, stay with central to
decide and do, with divisions consulted.

23
00:01:33.301 --> 00:01:36.677
And an encrypted DNS exception
that has no end date,

24
00:01:36.677 --> 00:01:41.181
or has outlived the agency's limit,
goes to the authorizing official,

25
00:01:41.181 --> 00:01:45.925
because at that point it's a risk
acceptance, not a configuration choice.

26
00:01:45.925 --> 00:01:48.155
Sources: Book 08, Chapter 09,

27
00:01:48.155 --> 00:01:54.642
"Decision rights and work are different
things," "The roles," "The responsibility

28
00:01:54.642 --> 00:02:00.890
matrix"; Infoblox NIOS approval
workflows; Universal DDI access policies.
